# Neutralise Pico's bogus boot-control HAL declaration WITHOUT touching /vendor.
#
# /vendor/manifest.xml declares android.hardware.boot@1.0::IBootControl/default,
# but the vendor ships no implementation (no /vendor/bin/hw binary, no
# bootctrl.*.so) and the device is not A/B at all - no slot_suffix, no _a/_b
# partitions. Android 8.1 never called IBootControl so the false claim was free.
#
# Android 10 vold calls it on every boot from cp_needsCheckpoint(). libhidl blocks
# forever on an interface that VINTF declares but nobody registers, so vold's main
# thread never returns, its mutex is held, and every later remountUid/setListener
# piles up behind it. StorageManagerService then trips the watchdog and
# system_server is killed roughly every 80 seconds, forever.
#
# Rather than editing the stock vendor partition, shadow the one file with a copy
# that has the <hal> block removed. The partition stays byte-identical; only the
# mounted view changes. Must land before anything queries VINTF.

on early-init
    mount none /system/etc/pn2/vendor_manifest.xml /vendor/manifest.xml bind
